---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "unjury protein powder",
      "unjury bariatric protein",
      "unjury review",
      "protein powder after bariatric surgery"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Weight Loss Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-weight-loss/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Unjury Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Unjury is a whey isolate powder designed with surgical and medical patients in mind: 21 g of protein in a small scoop, savory flavors and an unflavored version that disappears into food. Here is what the label delivers, and what it costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-is-unjury">Who Unjury Is</a>
      <a href="#label">The Label</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Range, Ranked</a>
      <a href="#bariatric">Unjury After Bariatric Surgery</a>
      <a href="#flavors">Choosing a Flavor</a>
      <a href="#mixing">How to Mix It</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#vs">Unjury vs Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Unjury is a well-made, narrowly focused protein powder. Every whey flavor lists 21 g of protein from whey isolate in a scoop of about 24 to 31 g, with 90 to 110 calories and 2 g of sugar or less, and the range includes savory soups and an unflavored version you can stir into food. That makes it unusually practical for people who can only manage small volumes, including many bariatric patients. It is not cheap per gram, it carries no sport certification, and none of this replaces the plan your surgical team or dietitian gives you.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict: Small Scoop, Clean Numbers, Premium Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unjury's appeal is the label. The Unflavored powder lists a 24 g scoop with about 90 calories, 21 g of protein, zero carbohydrate, zero fat and about 35 mg of sodium, made from whey protein isolate and soy lecithin. The flavored shakes stay close to that, with Vanilla at about 100 calories and Chocolate at about 110, each with about 2 g of sugar. Almost all of what you swallow is protein, which matters most when you can only swallow a little.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second strength is range. Alongside the sweet flavors, Unjury sells savory Chicken Soup, French Onion and Beef and Herb powders, plus Lemon Lime and Strawberry. For anyone who is tired of sweet shakes, or who finds them hard to tolerate, a warm savory protein drink is a genuinely useful option, and few mainstream brands offer one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The drawbacks are price and certification. A 15-serving tub lists at about $30 on Unjury's site, which works out to roughly $2.40 per 25 g of protein, approximately, before subscription discounts. That is well above mainstream isolates. And Unjury lists no third-party certification such as NSF or Informed Sport; it relies on its own quality claims and on a statement that it is recommended at top-rated hospitals, none of which it names.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our label-based verdict: Unjury is a good choice when small serving size, low sugar, savory options or easy mixing into food are what you need, and an expensive one when they are not. We built this review from Unjury's labels and published claims; we did not taste or test the products, and nothing here is medical advice. If you have had or are preparing for bariatric surgery, your surgical team or registered dietitian should approve the product and the amount.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A practical, low-sugar whey isolate with a niche price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Unjury earns its reputation with 21 g of isolate protein in a small scoop and savory flavors. It costs more per gram than mainstream isolates, so buy it for its format, not its value.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-unjury" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Unjury Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unjury describes itself as medical-quality protein founded more than 20 years ago by a clinical dietitian. It is based in Middletown, Connecticut, and its site also carries a second brand, Bariatric Fusion, plus vitamins such as bariatric calcium citrate soft chews and multivitamins. The pairing tells you who the company is designing for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein range is wider than most bariatric brands. Unjury lists eight whey isolate powders in 15-serving tubs: Chocolate, Vanilla, Unflavored, Lemon Lime, Strawberry, Chicken Soup, French Onion and Beef and Herb. Most of those also come as single-serve stick packets in boxes of 20. There are two pea-protein plant powders in Chocolate and Unflavored, plus ready-to-drink shakes and clinical nutrition drinks that we do not cover here.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unjury's marketing leans on clinical credibility: it says the products are recommended at America's top-rated hospitals and at leading cancer centers. Unjury does not name the hospitals on the pages we read, so treat that as a brand claim. Its site also carries the standard statement that its claims have not been evaluated by the FDA.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unjury sells primarily direct from its own site, with subscriptions and a practitioner program. We could not confirm a current brand-run Amazon US listing for each flavor, so the buttons on this page run an Amazon search for the product name. If you buy on Amazon, check that the seller is Unjury or a retailer you trust, and compare the price with the brand site, which is often competitive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Label: 21 g of Whey Isolate, Not Much Else
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Unjury whey powder we checked uses whey protein isolate as its only protein source and lists 21 g of protein per scoop. Isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat found in milk, and Unjury's Chocolate label states zero grams of lactose. That is relevant because some people become more sensitive to lactose after certain surgeries, and an isolate is usually the better-tolerated whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Unflavored label is about as short as a protein powder gets: whey protein isolate and soy lecithin, which helps the powder disperse. The sweet flavors add fructose, natural and artificial flavors, xanthan gum, sea salt and two non-nutritive sweeteners, acesulfame potassium and sucralose. That combination keeps sugar at about 2 g per scoop. If you avoid artificial sweeteners, Unflavored and the savory flavors are the ones to look at.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Flavor</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sodium</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 35 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">28 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 65 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">31 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chicken Soup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 770 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The savory flavors are where you need to watch sodium. Chicken Soup lists about 770 mg per scoop, roughly a third of the daily value, because it is seasoned like a broth with salt, yeast extract, onion and garlic powder. That is normal for a soup, but it is a lot for a supplement, especially if you drink it more than once a day or are on a sodium-restricted diet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Twenty-one grams is slightly below the 25 g serving many sports powders use. For most people that difference is minor, but if you are aiming for a specific daily target, count it as 21 g rather than rounding up. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">protein needs guide</a> explains how targets are usually set, and our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate explainer</a> covers why isolate is easier on lactose-sensitive stomachs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Unjury Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Unjury's whey powders share one base, whey protein isolate, and differ mainly in flavor, sweeteners and sodium. We rank four of them by versatility and label, then add two labelled alternatives from other brands. Figures are per Unjury's published labels at the time of writing and can change.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="bariatric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Using Unjury After Bariatric Surgery
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NOT MEDICAL ADVICE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Your surgical team sets the plan.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Protein targets, the timing of each diet stage, which products are allowed and how much fluid to take all vary by procedure and by program. Use this section as background and follow your bariatric surgeon and registered dietitian.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Protein matters a great deal after weight-loss surgery. Food intake falls sharply, and preserving lean tissue while weight comes off depends on getting enough protein in a very small volume. Published bariatric nutrition guidelines commonly cite a minimum of around 60 g of protein a day, with some programs setting higher targets based on body size, but your team will set your number. In the early stages, many patients rely on protein drinks to reach it.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the niche Unjury was built for. A scoop of about 24 to 31 g of powder delivers 21 g of protein, so even a small drink covers a meaningful share of the day. The unflavored version can be stirred into foods your program allows at each stage, such as broth, yogurt or puree, which can be easier than forcing down another sweet shake. And the savory soups give a warm option when sweet drinks become hard to face.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some programs also ask patients to keep sugar per serving low, partly to reduce the risk of dumping syndrome after certain procedures. Unjury's flavored powders list about 2 g of sugar per scoop and the unflavored and soup versions list zero, which is why they often fit those limits. Check the label against the specific number your team gives you, because rules differ.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two cautions. First, the savory flavors are high in sodium, so ask your team whether they fit your plan. Second, Unjury does not carry an independent certification such as NSF Certified for Sport, so you are relying on the brand's own quality controls. If your program recommends a specific product, follow that recommendation. For a broader overview of supplement safety, see our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="flavors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sweet, Savory or Unflavored: Choosing a Flavor
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Unflavored</strong> is the most versatile and the leanest. It has no sweeteners, no sugar and the lowest sodium in the range at about 35 mg per scoop. Unjury itself says it does not recommend mixing Unflavored into plain water, and suggests a strongly flavored drink instead, which tells you it tastes of something. It is best stirred into foods and drinks you already like. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder guide</a> compares it with other plain powders.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chocolate</strong> and <strong>Vanilla</strong> are the classic shake flavors, at about 110 and 100 calories respectively, each with about 2 g of sugar from fructose and sweetened further with sucralose and acesulfame potassium. Customer reviews on Unjury's site mention the chocolate often, and vanilla works as a base for fruit smoothies. Both mix with milk or a milk alternative if your plan allows it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Chicken Soup, French Onion and Beef and Herb</strong> are the range's real differentiator. At about 90 calories and 21 g of protein per scoop for Chicken Soup, they turn a warm drink into a protein serving. They are high in sodium and, like every Unjury whey, must not be mixed with liquid hotter than about 140 degrees Fahrenheit, so they are warm drinks rather than piping-hot soups.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Lemon Lime and Strawberry</strong> are fruit-style flavors for people who find milky shakes heavy. We could not confirm their full panels in time for this review, so check the label before you buy. If you prefer a fully juice-like drink, a clear whey isolate may suit you better; our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">clear protein powder guide</a> covers those.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because flavor tolerance can change, especially after surgery, starting with stick packets or a single tub before committing to a subscription is sensible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Mix Unjury Without Clumps
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most important instruction on Unjury's labels is temperature. The brand says not to use its powders in liquids hotter than 140 degrees Fahrenheit, because the proteins clump. That applies to the savory soups too: heat the water, let it cool to warm, then stir in the powder. Adding powder to boiling broth or fresh coffee will give you lumps and an unpleasant texture.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cold drinks, add the liquid first and the powder second, then shake or whisk. Unjury's labels suggest mixing a scoop into about 8 fl oz of liquid for the soups. If you can only manage small sips, you can make a thicker drink with less liquid and sip it over a longer period, as long as your team is comfortable with that approach.
          </p>

          <p>
            For food, Unflavored works best stirred into something that is already cool or warm rather than hot: yogurt, cottage cheese, pudding, mashed potato or lukewarm soup. Stir it in a little at a time so it does not clump, and taste as you go. A scoop adds 21 g of protein and very little volume, which is the whole point.
          </p>

          <p>
            A small electric frother or shaker bottle with a mixing ball handles most of the work. Blenders work too, but make more foam, which can feel filling before you have finished the serving.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
