        <div id="vs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Unjury vs Premier, Isopure and Plant Options
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Premier Protein shakes</strong> are the most common grocery-store alternative. They list about 30 g of protein, about 160 calories and about 1 g of sugar per 11 fl oz carton, and they cost little per gram when bought by the case. They need no mixing, but the carton volume is much larger than a small Unjury drink, which matters if your capacity is limited. Our <a href="/premier-protein-powder-review/">Premier Protein review</a> covers the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Isopure Zero Carb</strong> is the closest powdered alternative on the label: about 25 g of protein for roughly 100 to 110 calories with essentially no carbohydrate, from pure whey isolate. It is sold in larger tubs and typically costs less per 25 g. It lacks Unjury's savory flavors. See our <a href="/isopure-protein-powder-review/">Isopure review</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Quest Protein Powder</strong> is another low-sugar grocery option, but it blends whey isolate with casein, which gives a thicker shake and a little more lactose. It suits general dieters better than people who need the smallest, smoothest drink possible. Our <a href="/quest-protein-powder-review/">Quest review</a> compares every flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plant proteins</strong> make sense if you cannot have dairy. Unjury's own pea powders are an option, and our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein guide</a> covers others. Expect more calories and carbohydrate per gram of protein than with whey isolate.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip Unjury
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Budget-first, long-term users.</strong> If you are past the stage where small-volume, specialised products matter, a large tub of a mainstream whey isolate will usually deliver the same protein for less.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drug-tested athletes.</strong> Unjury lists no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification. Choose a certified product for your exact flavor instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>People avoiding sucralose or acesulfame potassium.</strong> The sweet flavors contain both. Stick to Unflavored or the soups, or choose another brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anyone on a sodium-restricted diet</strong> should be careful with the savory flavors, which list several hundred milligrams of sodium per scoop. Unflavored is the low-sodium option.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Anyone with soy or milk allergy.</strong> Every Unjury whey powder contains milk and soy lecithin. And anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant, or who has had surgery should agree any protein supplement with their doctor or dietitian first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing Unjury into hot liquid</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unjury says its powders clump in liquid hotter than about 140 degrees Fahrenheit. That includes the savory soups. Heat water, let it cool to warm, then stir the powder in. Adding a scoop to boiling broth or fresh coffee wastes the serving and leaves an unpleasant texture.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overlooking sodium in the savory flavors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chicken Soup lists about 770 mg of sodium per scoop, roughly a third of the daily value. Two servings a day adds up quickly. If you are on a sodium-restricted plan, check with your doctor or dietitian, and use Unflavored, at about 35 mg, as your everyday option.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting a scoop as 25 g of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every Unjury whey scoop lists 21 g of protein, not 25 g. If you round up, you will fall short of a daily target, which matters most when targets are tight and volumes small. Track the real figure, or use a slightly larger scoop if your team agrees.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a product without your surgical team</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bariatric programs differ in their protein targets, sugar limits and timing of each diet stage. A product that suits one program may not suit another. Ask your surgeon or registered dietitian which powders they approve before you buy, rather than relying on brand claims or reviews.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a subscription before testing flavors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Taste tolerance can change, particularly after surgery, and a flavor you liked before may become hard to drink. Try stick packets or a single tub first, then subscribe once you know what works. A full tub you cannot finish is the most expensive protein there is.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Unjury protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For its purpose, yes. Every Unjury whey powder lists 21 g of protein from whey isolate in a small scoop, with about 90 to 110 calories and 2 g of sugar or less, and the range includes savory and unflavored options. It costs more per gram than mainstream isolates and carries no third-party certification, so it suits specific needs better than general use.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in Unjury?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Each Unjury whey powder flavor we checked lists 21 g of protein per scoop, with scoop sizes from about 24 g for Unflavored to about 31 g for Chocolate. The pea-protein Chocolate plant powder lists about 20 g per serving. Figures are per Unjury's labels at the time of writing and may change, so check the tub you buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Unjury good after bariatric surgery?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unjury is designed with surgical patients in mind, and its small scoop, low sugar, isolate base and savory flavors suit many post-surgery plans. But programs differ in protein targets, sugar limits and when each product is allowed. This is not medical advice: ask your bariatric surgeon or registered dietitian before using Unjury or any protein supplement.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Unjury contain lactose?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unjury uses whey protein isolate, which is filtered to remove most lactose, and its Chocolate label claims zero grams of lactose. That makes it usually better tolerated than whey concentrate by lactose-sensitive people. If you are highly sensitive or have a milk allergy, check with your doctor; all Unjury whey powders contain milk and soy lecithin.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can you mix Unjury into hot food or drinks?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Only warm, not hot. Unjury's labels say not to use the powders in liquids hotter than about 140 degrees Fahrenheit, because the protein clumps. For soups or coffee, let the liquid cool to warm before stirring in the powder. Unflavored is the best version for mixing into foods such as yogurt, pudding or lukewarm soup.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What sweeteners does Unjury use?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The sweet flavors, such as Chocolate and Vanilla, contain fructose, acesulfame potassium and sucralose, which keeps sugar at about 2 g per scoop. Unflavored has no sweeteners at all, and the Chicken Soup label lists none. The pea-protein plant Chocolate uses organic cane sugar and stevia. Check each label if you avoid a specific sweetener.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

